answer.worked:
Let $u = 2\theta - \frac{\pi}{3}$.
Since $0 \leq \theta \leq 2\pi$:
$$\begin{align*}
0 &\leq 2\theta \leq 4\pi \\
-\frac{\pi}{3} &\leq 2\theta - \frac{\pi}{3} \leq \frac{11\pi}{3}
\end{align*}$$
Now solve the equation:
$$\begin{align*}
\sqrt{2}\cos u - 1 &= 0 \\
\cos u &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
\cos u &= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
\end{align*}$$
In the required interval, the solutions are:
$$\begin{align*}
u &= -\frac{\pi}{4},\ \frac{\pi}{4},\ \frac{7\pi}{4},\ \frac{9\pi}{4}
\end{align*}$$
Substituting $u = 2\theta - \frac{\pi}{3}$ gives:
$$\begin{align*}
\theta &= \frac{\pi}{24},\ \frac{7\pi}{24},\ \frac{25\pi}{24},\ \frac{31\pi}{24}
\end{align*}$$

